<UserControl x:Class="SteamRouteTool.Views.HowToView"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
    <StackPanel MaxWidth="820" HorizontalAlignment="Left">
      <TextBlock Text="How to use" Style="{StaticResource PageTitle}" Margin="0,0,0,16" />

      <Border Style="{StaticResource Card}">
        <StackPanel>
          <TextBlock Text="What it does" Style="{StaticResource SectionTitle}" />
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">
            Valve games like Counter-Strike 2, Dota 2 and Deadlock connect you to game servers through Valve's relay network (Steam Datagram Relay).
            Steam Route Tool lets you block relays in specific locations with Windows Firewall, so matchmaking only puts you on the servers you want, for example only nearby ones.
          </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <Border Style="{StaticResource Card}">
        <StackPanel>
          <TextBlock Text="Getting started" Style="{StaticResource SectionTitle}" />
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">1. Pick your game at the top of the Routes page. The route list comes from Steam and is always current.</TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">2. Wait for the pings to fill in. Green is under 50 ms, amber under 100 ms, red above.</TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">3. Tick <Bold>Blocked</Bold> on every route you don't want to play on. The firewall rule is created immediately.</TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">4. Start (or restart) matchmaking in the game. Blocked locations aren't used anymore.</TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">5. When you're done, use <Bold>Unblock all</Bold> to remove every rule, or leave them: they stay until you change them. To block only while the app is open, turn on <Bold>Unblock all routes when closing</Bold> in Settings.</TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <Border Style="{StaticResource Card}">
        <StackPanel>
          <TextBlock Text="Blocking routes" Style="{StaticResource SectionTitle}" />
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">
            <Bold>Only play in one region:</Bold> choose the region (for example Europe) in the region filter and click <Bold>Block all except shown</Bold>.
            Every other route is blocked and the shown ones are unblocked. This works with the search box too.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">
            <Bold>Individual relays:</Bold> most locations have several relay servers. Click the arrow next to a route to show them and block single relays.
            A route with only some relays blocked shows a filled checkbox (■).
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">
            <Bold>Sorting and filtering:</Bold> click a column header to sort by name, region, relay count, ping or blocked state.
            Use the search box, the region and status filters, or turn off <Bold>Group by region</Bold> for one flat list.
          </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <Border Style="{StaticResource Card}">
        <StackPanel>
          <TextBlock Text="Game profiles" Style="{StaticResource SectionTitle}" />
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">
            Each game remembers its own blocked routes. When you switch games, that game's saved selection is applied to the firewall automatically.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">
            Valve's games share the same relay servers, and firewall rules apply to your whole PC. Blocking a route for one game therefore blocks it for every game until you switch profiles or unblock it.
          </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <Border Style="{StaticResource Card}">
        <StackPanel>
          <TextBlock Text="Pings" Style="{StaticResource SectionTitle}" />
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">
            Greyed values are from your last session and are replaced as fresh results arrive. A collapsed route shows the best ping of its relays.
            <Bold>no reply</Bold> means none of the tried relays answered; <Bold>blocked</Bold> routes aren't pinged because their pings are blocked too.
            Click any ping to measure it again.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">
            Pings are sent quickly in a first round and anything that didn't answer is retried one at a time. Some routers (flood protection) and ISPs drop replies when many pings are sent at once; on such networks the list fills in over 10–20 seconds.
          </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <Border Style="{StaticResource Card}">
        <StackPanel>
          <TextBlock Text="Troubleshooting" Style="{StaticResource SectionTitle}" />
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">
            <Bold>"Couldn't update the Windows Firewall rules":</Bold> the tool needs administrator rights (Windows asks when it starts) and the Windows Defender Firewall service must be running.
            Third-party firewalls that replace Windows Firewall aren't supported.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">
            <Bold>Still connected to a blocked location:</Bold> blocking only affects new connections. Leave the match and search again.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">
            <Bold>Seeing the rules:</Bold> open Windows Defender Firewall (Settings → Open Windows Firewall) → Outbound Rules. The rules are named SteamRouteTool-UDP/TCP/ICMP-&lt;route&gt; and grouped under SteamRouteTool.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">
            <Bold>Windows protected your PC:</Bold> the download isn't code-signed yet, so SmartScreen may warn the first time. Choose More info, then Run anyway.
          </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <Border Style="{StaticResource Card}">
        <StackPanel>
          <TextBlock Text="Removing the tool" Style="{StaticResource SectionTitle}" />
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource BodyText}">
            Click <Bold>Unblock all</Bold> first, so no firewall rules are left behind. Then delete the program and, if you like, its settings folder:
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBox Text="{Binding SettingsFolder, Mode=OneWay}" IsReadOnly="True" BorderThickness="0" Background="Transparent" FontFamily="Consolas" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>
    </StackPanel>
  </ScrollViewer>
</UserControl>
